## Proof

Let $G$ be a finite abelian group with $|G|=n$. Since $G$ is abelian, every irreducible character is one-dimensional, so the number of irreducible characters equals $|G|=n$. Thus the character table $X$ is an $n\times n$ matrix whose $(i,j)$-entry is $X_{ij}=\chi_i(g_j)$, where $\chi_1,\dots,\chi_n$ are the irreducible characters and $g_1,\dots,g_n$ are the elements of $G$.

**Row orthogonality.** The character orthogonality relation (first kind) states that for irreducible characters $\chi_i,\chi_j$,

$$\sum_{g\in G} \chi_i(g)\overline{\chi_j(g)} = |G|\,\delta_{ij} = n\,\delta_{ij}.$$

In matrix notation, this is precisely

$$X\,X^{*} = n\,I_n,$$

where $X^{*}=\overline{X}^{\mathsf T}$ is the conjugate transpose of $X$.

**Determinant computation.** Taking determinants of both sides,

$$\det(X\,X^{*}) = \det(n\,I_n) = n^n.$$

Since $\det(X^{*})=\overline{\det(X)}$, the left side is

$$\det(X)\,\overline{\det(X)} = |\det(X)|^2.$$

Therefore

$$|\det(X)|^2 = n^n,$$

and taking square roots (noting $|\det(X)|\ge 0$),

$$|\det(X)| = n^{n/2}.$$

**Verification.** For $G=\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$ ($n=2$), the character table is $\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&-1\end{pmatrix}$ with $|\det|=2=2^{2/2}$. ✓ For $G=\mathbb{Z}/3\mathbb{Z}$ ($n=3$), the character table is the $3\times 3$ DFT matrix, with $|\det|=3^{3/2}=3\sqrt{3}$. ✓

The answer depends only on $n=|G|$ and not on the structure of $G$, since the orthogonality relation holds for every finite group and depends only on $|G|$.

$$\boxed{|\det(X)| = n^{n/2}}$$

